==English==

===Etymology===
A twentieth-century borrowing of [[Ancient Greek]] ''[[ἐμπάθεια]]'' (''empatheia''), literally ‘passion’ (formed from ''[[εν-]]'' ''en-'', ‘in, at’ + ''[[πάθος]]'' ''pathos'' ‘feeling’), coined by [[w:Edward B. Titchener|Edward Bradford Titchener]] to translate German ''[[Einfühlung]]''. The modern Greek word [[εμπάθεια]] has an opposite meaning denoting strong negative feelings and prejudice against someone.

===Noun===
{{wikipedia}}
{{en-noun|-}}

# the intellectual identification of the thoughts, feelings, or state of another person
# capacity to understand another person's [[point of view]] or the result of such understanding

